cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  powercycle: search, transformation and independent verification of
  power-sum Hamiltonian cycles
message: >-
  If you use this software or the witnesses it publishes, please cite it
  using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Nouvier
    given-names: Frédéric
    email: frnouv.math@gmail.com
license: MIT OR Apache-2.0
version: 0.2.0
abstract: >-
  For integers k >= 2 and n >= 1, the power-sum graph G_k(n) has vertex set
  {1, ..., n} and joins two distinct vertices whose sum is a perfect k-th
  power. This repository contains the tooling used to search for Hamiltonian
  cycles in these graphs, together with curated, independently verifiable
  witnesses and the lower-bound certificates that establish minimality.
keywords:
  - Hamiltonian cycle
  - power-sum graph
  - square-sum problem
  - perfect powers
  - SAT solving
  - combinatorial search
  - reproducible computation
references:
  - type: data
    title: witness/k5-n246877 — minimal Hamiltonian witness for k = 5
    authors:
      - family-names: Nouvier
        given-names: Frédéric
    license: CC0-1.0
    notes: >-
      Establishes n_min(5) = 246877, together with the lower-bound certificate
      ruling out every smaller n.
  - type: database
    title: >-
      A115418: shortest n-th-power loop, a circular permutation of 1..m whose
      consecutive sums are perfect n-th powers
    authors:
      - family-names: Tauraso
        given-names: Roberto
    year: 2006
    url: "https://oeis.org/A115418"
    notes: >-
      Lists n_min(k) for k = 1..4 (2, 32, 473, 9641). The values for k = 3 and
      4 predate this repository, which certifies their minimality and adds
      k = 5 and 6.
  - type: website
    title: "Puzzle 311: Sum to a cube"
    authors:
      - family-names: Rivera
        given-names: Carlos
    url: "http://www.primepuzzles.net/puzzles/puzz_311.htm"
    notes: >-
      Giovanni Resta's cubic loop of length 473, found by reduction to a
      travelling salesman problem.
